[2025-12-14 06:38:14,508: WARNING/ForkPoolWorker-3] [LLM] ✓ Summary generated using: ChatCompletion(id='chatcmpl-CmZ5sQJuMhLLpkLfq2TjYlnNMoB0y', choices=[Choice(finish_reason='stop', index=0, logprobs=None, message=ChatCompletionMessage(content='# Summary of Clinical Trials Related to Malaria\n\nThis summary consolidates data from multiple clinical trials investigating various interventions, treatments, and preventive strategies for malaria. The trials span different phases and study types, providing insights into vaccine development, drug efficacy, vector control, and behavioral interventions.\n\n---\n\n## Trials by Status and Phase\n\n### Phase 4 (Post-Marketing and Late-Stage Studies)\n- **NCT07038837**  \n  *Title:* Comparison of Two Strategies for Administering the R21-Matrix M Vaccine in Seasonal Malaria Transmission in Chad  \n  *Status:* Not yet recruiting (Start: July 2025)  \n  *Focus:* Evaluates vaccine administration strategies targeting seasonal malaria.\n\n- **NCT03765931**  \n  *Title:* Comparative Efficacy of Single-dose Doxycycline Versus Standard 5-Day Amoxicillin Treatment  \n  *Status:* Completed (Start: July 2016)  \n  *Focus:* Treatment efficacy for fever, relevant to malaria symptom management.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 3 (Pivotal Efficacy Studies)\n- **NCT06179732**  \n  *Title:* Field Evaluations of Innovative Tools for Vector-borne Disease Control in Conflict-affected Communities  \n  *Status:* Completed (Start: June 2023)  \n  *Focus:* Vector control tools in challenging settings, addressing malaria transmission reduction.\n\n- **NCT00709969**  \n  *Title:* Evaluation of Safety and Efficacy of Artemether-Lumefantrine Tablets in African Infants and Children With Uncomplicated P. falciparum Malaria  \n  *Status:* Completed (Start: July 2002)  \n  *Focus:* Standard antimalarial drug efficacy in pediatric populations.\n\n- **NCT05946642**  \n  *Title:* Innovative Intermittent Preventive Treatment Approaches to Reduce Malaria Burden in School-age Children in Burkina Faso  \n  *Status:* Unknown (Start: July 2023)  \n  *Focus:* Preventive treatment strategies targeting school-age children.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 2 and 2/3 (Early to Mid-Stage Efficacy and Safety)\n- **NCT02509481**  \n  *Title:* Repeat Ivermectin Mass Drug Administrations for Control of Malaria: a Pilot Safety and Efficacy Study  \n  *Status:* Completed (Start: June 2015)  \n  *Focus:* Mass drug administration using ivermectin for malaria and lymphatic filariasis control.\n\n- **NCT03167242**  \n  *Title:* Efficacy and Safety of KAF156 in Combination With Lumefantrine in Adults and Children With Uncomplicated P. falciparum Malaria  \n  *Status:* Completed (Start: August 2017)  \n  *Focus:* Evaluates a novel antimalarial combination therapy.\n\n- **NCT04333628**  \n  *Title:* Chloroquine for Mild Symptomatic and Asymptomatic COVID-19  \n  *Status:* Terminated (Start: June 2020)  \n  *Note:* Although related to COVID-19, included due to chloroquine’s antimalarial relevance.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 1 and 1/2 (Early Safety and Immunogenicity)\n- **NCT01082341**  \n  *Title:* Immunization of Human Volunteers With P. vivax Irradiated Sporozoites  \n  *Status:* Completed (Start: June 2012)  \n  *Focus:* Early vaccine development targeting P. vivax malaria.\n\n- **NCT00149383**  \n  *Title:* Safety and Efficacy Study of Adjunctive Rosiglitazone in the Treatment of Uncomplicated Falciparum Malaria  \n  *Status:* Completed (Start: December 2004)  \n  *Focus:* Adjunctive therapy to improve treatment outcomes.\n\n- **NCT03599596**  \n  *Title:* Monthly Versus Two Doses of Ante-Natal Intermittent Preventive Treatment With Sulphadoxine-Pyrimethamine  \n  *Status:* Unknown (Start: September 2018)  \n  *Focus:* Preventive treatment in pregnancy.\n\n---\n\n### Observational Studies\n- **NCT00341666**  \n  *Title:* Immune Response Regulation in People Infected Concurrently With Malarial and Filarial Parasites  \n  *Status:* Completed (Start: April 2006)  \n  *Focus:* Understanding immune interactions in co-infections.\n\n- **NCT05072613**  \n  *Title:* Monitoring Pregnant Women for Antimalarial Drug Resistance  \n  *Status:* Completed (Start: November 2021)  \n  *Focus:* Surveillance of drug resistance in pregnancy.\n\n---\n\n### Other Notable Studies\n- **NCT04570241**  \n  *Title:* Mobilization at the Level of Schools on Behavioral Change in Favor of the Fight Against Malaria in Mbandjock  \n  *Status:* Unknown (Start: January 2017)  \n  *Focus:* Behavioral interventions targeting malaria prevention in school settings.\n\n- **NCT04416945**  \n  *Title:* Targeting High Risk Populations With Enhanced Reactive Case Detection in Southern Lao PDR  \n  *Status:* Unknown (Start: September 2020)  \n  *Focus:* Case detection strategies for P. falciparum and P. vivax malaria.\n\n- **NCT04053907**  \n  *Title:* P. falciparum Infection Dynamics and Transmission to Inform Elimination  \n  *Status:* Unknown (Start: August 2019)  \n  *Focus:* Transmission studies to support malaria elimination efforts.\n\n- **NCT01292707**  \n  *Title:* A Cluster-randomised Trial of Interventions to Improve Antimalarial Prescribing With Malaria Tests  \n  *Status:* Completed (Start: February 2011)  \n  *Focus:* Improving diagnostic-driven treatment practices.\n\n- **NCT00658450**  \n  *Title:* Rehabilitation Program for Cognitive Deficits in Ugandan Children After Cerebral Malaria  \n  *Status:* Completed (Start: February 2008)  \n  *Focus:* Post-malaria cognitive rehabilitation.\n\n---\n\n## Practical Insights\n\n- **Vaccine Development:** The upcoming Phase 4 trial (NCT07038837) of the R21-Matrix M vaccine in Chad represents a promising advance in seasonal malaria vaccination strategies.\n\n- **Drug Therapies:** Multiple completed Phase 3 and 2 trials confirm the efficacy and safety of established antimalarials like artemether-lumefantrine and explore new combinations such as KAF156 with lumefantrine.\n\n- **Mass Drug Administration:** Ivermectin mass administration (NCT02509481) shows potential as a vector control adjunct, especially in co-endemic areas with lymphatic filariasis.\n\n- **Behavioral and Vector Control:** Trials addressing vector control tools and community mobilization highlight the importance of integrated approaches beyond pharmacological interventions.\n\n- **Pregnancy and Pediatric Focus:** Several studies target vulnerable populations, including pregnant women and children, emphasizing tailored preventive and therapeutic strategies.\n\n- **Resistance Monitoring:** Ongoing surveillance of antimalarial drug resistance remains critical to maintaining treatment efficacy.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThe landscape of malaria clinical trials is broad, encompassing vaccine development, drug efficacy, vector control, behavioral interventions, and resistance monitoring. While many trials have been completed providing valuable data, several important studies are ongoing or yet to start, indicating continued efforts to reduce malaria burden globally. Users interested in malaria interventions should monitor upcoming Phase 4 vaccine trials and innovative preventive approaches targeting high-risk populations.', refusal=None, role='assistant', annotations=[], audio=None, function_call=None, tool_calls=None))], created=1765690680, model='gpt-4.1-mini-2025-04-14', object='chat.completion', service_tier='default', system_fingerprint='fp_d0c93c37b1', usage=CompletionUsage(completion_tokens=1565, prompt_tokens=2973, total_tokens=4538, completion_tokens_details=CompletionTokensDetails(accepted_prediction_tokens=0, audio_tokens=0, reasoning_tokens=0, rejected_prediction_tokens=0), prompt_tokens_details=PromptTokensDetails(audio_tokens=0, cached_tokens=0)))
